2026-11-06T20:40:05.2005310Z 
2026-11-06T20:40:05.2005660Z Using `clang`, `clang++`, etc., requires a CLT installation at `/Library/Developer/CommandLineTools`.
2026-11-06T20:40:05.2006400Z If you don't want to install the CLT, you can write appropriate configuration files pointing to your
2026-11-06T20:40:05.2006970Z SDK at ~/.config/clang.
2026-11-06T20:40:05.2007240Z 
2026-11-06T20:40:05.2007470Z To use the bundled libunwind please use the following LDFLAGS:
2026-11-06T20:40:05.2008150Z   LDFLAGS="-L/opt/homebrew/opt/llvm/lib/unwind -lunwind"
2026-11-06T20:40:05.2008490Z 
2026-11-06T20:40:05.2008640Z To use the bundled libc++ please use the following LDFLAGS:
2026-11-06T20:40:05.2009340Z   LDFLAGS="-L/opt/homebrew/opt/llvm/lib/c++ -L/opt/homebrew/opt/llvm/lib/unwind -lunwind"
2026-11-06T20:40:05.2009980Z Features newer than system libc++ will require the following define to enable
2026-11-06T20:40:05.2010530Z (support for this may be removed in a future major LLVM release):
2026-11-06T20:40:05.2011060Z   CPPFLAGS="-D_LIBCPP_DISABLE_AVAILABILITY"
2026-11-06T20:40:05.2011340Z 
2026-11-06T20:40:05.2011730Z NOTE: You probably want to use the libunwind and libc++ provided by macOS unless you know what you're doing.
2026-11-06T20:40:05.2012100Z 
2026-11-06T20:40:05.2012350Z llvm is keg-only, which means it was not symlinked into /opt/homebrew,
2026-11-06T20:40:05.2013000Z because macOS already provides this software and installing another version in
2026-11-06T20:40:05.2013480Z parallel can cause all kinds of trouble.
2026-11-06T20:40:05.2013750Z 
2026-11-06T20:40:05.2013960Z If you need to have llvm first in your PATH, run:
2026-11-06T20:40:05.2026660Z   printf '%s\n' export\ PATH\=/opt/homebrew/opt/llvm/bin:\$PATH >> /Users/runner/.bash_profile
2026-11-06T20:40:05.2028140Z 
